/site

# mypy
.mypy_cache/

# PyCharm
.idea

# an per-project ephemera (renders, intermediates, agent state)
examples/*/output/
examples/*/artifacts/
examples/*/assets/
examples/*/.an/
examples/*/.anima/
examples/*/an.toml
examples/*/anima.toml

# The promote_demo example's input SVG is the whole point of the example
# — keep it tracked even though examples/*/assets/ is otherwise ignored.
!examples/promote_demo/assets/
!examples/promote_demo/assets/characters/
!examples/promote_demo/assets/characters/raw_maya/
!examples/promote_demo/assets/characters/raw_maya/*.svg
# But re-exclude the promote target — it's regenerated by build.py.
examples/promote_demo/assets/characters/maya-promoted/

# Agent worktrees (adversarial reviewers run isolated here so they cannot
# mutate the shared checkout). Never committed, and excluded from tooling
# below or ruff/pytest walk three extra copies of the whole repo.
.claude/worktrees/

# Session-boundary handoffs. Local working notes, not project docs — anything
# that should outlive a session belongs in an issue or misc/docs/ instead.
.claude/handoffs/

# an bench corpus (an#38): the fixtures are COMMITTED WHOLE — their pixels have
# to be a function of the repo alone — so only the per-render ephemera is
# ignored here. Note there is deliberately no `assets/` rule: that is what
# `examples/*/assets/` has, and it is why the corpus does not live there.
misc/bench/corpus/*/output/
misc/bench/corpus/*/artifacts/
misc/bench/corpus/*/.an/
misc/bench/corpus/*/ir/
# …except `dialogue`'s IR, which is the fixture: the bench renders with
# `auto_audio=False`, so the visemes a speaking scene needs must already be in
# its committed `ir/scene.json` (offline provider, deterministic), exactly as
# `examples/single_character/ir/scene.json` does. Without this carve-out the
# scene would be mute on a clean checkout and its "mid-line" golden a lie (an#96).
!misc/bench/corpus/dialogue/ir/
!misc/bench/corpus/dialogue/ir/scene.json

# `misc/demos/build_demos.py`'s output. Regenerable in a couple of minutes from
# the committed script, and the media is published as release assets rather than
# committed — `misc/` ships in the sdist, so a megabyte of GIFs would ride along
# with every `pip install`.
misc/demos/out/
